The PostgreSQL benchmark shows that database retrieval is not a significant bottleneck for the enrichment pipeline. Spatially ordered retrieval scales particularly well: retrieving 1,000 events takes approximately 11 ms, 15,000 events about 19 ms, 50,000 events about 41 ms, and even 100,000 events remain below 100 ms. Random retrieval is substantially slower, although these measurements also include the computational cost of PostgreSQL’s ORDER BY random() and therefore should not be interpreted as a pure measure of scattered I/O.

These results indicate that CUDAHeat can efficiently retrieve large batches of events from PostgreSQL and that event-by-event processing should be avoided. Events should instead be retrieved in large batches ordered by grid_point_index, preserving spatial locality.

The same ordering should then be maintained when accessing the corresponding climatology and threshold data stored in Zarr. The previous Zarr benchmarks showed that spatially contiguous access is considerably faster than scattered access, whereas temporal length and temporal intermittency have a comparatively small effect on performance. Consequently, PostgreSQL retrieval, Zarr storage layout, and the subsequent NumPy/CuPy enrichment operations should all follow the same spatial ordering.

The next optimization step should therefore focus on Zarr chunking. Different chunk sizes along the point dimension should be benchmarked, for example 256, 512, 1024, 2048, and 4096 points per chunk, while keeping the full climatological time dimension together where practical. The objective is to identify the chunk size that best matches the large, spatially ordered batches produced by PostgreSQL.

The resulting strategy is to retrieve large spatially ordered batches from PostgreSQL, read the corresponding contiguous blocks from Zarr, perform the enrichment calculations using vectorized NumPy or CuPy operations, and finally write the derived metrics back to PostgreSQL in bulk. This keeps spatial locality consistent throughout the entire enrichment pipeline and minimizes unnecessary storage reads, chunk decompression, and per-event overhead.